1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,800
From Graves to Gardens: How
Singapore Makes Room for Its Dead

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:09,111
By 1985, Singapore had
cleared 21 cemeteries

3
00:00:09,191 --> 00:00:12,603
and exhumed about 120,000 graves to

4
00:00:12,683 --> 00:00:16,575
make room for flats, roads and the MRT.

5
00:00:16,575 --> 00:00:19,703
The clearing went on after that: Bidadari,

6
00:00:19,783 --> 00:00:24,820
once the island's largest cemetery,
was emptied in the early 2000s,

7
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:29,100
and today even a new grave
is leased for only 15 years.

8
00:00:29,100 --> 00:00:33,326
Over the same decades, Singaporeans
changed how they lay their

9
00:00:33,406 --> 00:00:38,187
dead to rest, from burial to cremation,
from cemeteries to columbaria,

10
00:00:38,267 --> 00:00:42,225
and now to gardens where the
ashes go back into the soil.

11
00:00:42,225 --> 00:00:47,260
The first European cemetery in
British Singapore was on the

12
00:00:47,340 --> 00:00:52,975
slope of Fort Canning, used from
1823 until it filled up in 1865.

13
00:00:52,975 --> 00:00:56,554
Its replacement was a Christian cemetery

14
00:00:56,634 --> 00:01:00,304
on Bukit Timah Road, which took its first

15
00:01:00,384 --> 00:01:04,225
burial in April 1865 and its last in 1907.

16
00:01:04,225 --> 00:01:08,921
When that ground was finally
cleared around 1971, a dozen of its

17
00:01:09,001 --> 00:01:14,150
gravestones were moved to Fort
Canning Green, where they still stand.

18
00:01:14,150 --> 00:01:17,900
The town's municipal cemeteries came next.

19
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:20,728
Bidadari, off Upper Serangoon Road,

20
00:01:20,808 --> 00:01:25,547
opened on the 1st of January
1908 with Christian sections,

21
00:01:25,627 --> 00:01:29,200
and later Muslim, Hindu
and Sinhalese ones.

22
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:33,258
Bukit Brown, off Lornie
Road, opened on the 1st

23
00:01:33,338 --> 00:01:38,365
of January 1922 as the municipal
cemetery for the Chinese,

24
00:01:38,445 --> 00:01:41,350
and grew to about 100,000 graves.

25
00:01:41,350 --> 00:01:45,298
Alongside them were dozens
of private cemeteries run

26
00:01:45,378 --> 00:01:48,474
by Chinese clan and dialect associations,

27
00:01:48,554 --> 00:01:53,587
the largest of them Peck San Theng,
the Cantonese and Hakka burial

28
00:01:53,667 --> 00:01:57,850
ground founded in 1870 on
the land that is now Bishan.

29
00:01:57,850 --> 00:02:02,470
Singapore's Jews buried their
dead first off Orchard Road and,

30
00:02:02,550 --> 00:02:07,548
from 1904, in a cemetery on Thomson
Road, where Sir Manasseh Meyer,

31
00:02:07,628 --> 00:02:12,025
whom I wrote about in an earlier
post, was buried in 1930.

32
00:02:12,025 --> 00:02:16,450
Funerals at these cemeteries
could be large public events.

33
00:02:16,450 --> 00:02:20,545
In January 1953, a newspaper
photographer recorded the

34
00:02:20,625 --> 00:02:23,483
burial of Ng Thian Eng at Bukit Brown,

35
00:02:23,563 --> 00:02:28,975
with mourners gathered round the
open grave as the coffin was lowered.

36
00:02:28,975 --> 00:02:33,328
As early as 1950, the colonial
government set up a committee,

37
00:02:33,408 --> 00:02:35,945
headed by the Commissioner of Lands,

38
00:02:36,025 --> 00:02:39,150
to look into the problem
of burial grounds.

39
00:02:39,150 --> 00:02:43,850
Its report, tabled in the
Legislative Council in May 1952,

40
00:02:43,930 --> 00:02:48,630
found 182 licensed or private
burial grounds in Singapore,

41
00:02:48,710 --> 00:02:51,927
including seven run by the City Council,

42
00:02:52,007 --> 00:02:56,375
and 26 more so small they
could no longer be located.

43
00:02:56,375 --> 00:03:01,896
Graves took up nearly 1,730 acres
of land, and the Tiong Bahru flats,

44
00:03:01,976 --> 00:03:05,143
the Maxwell Road market and parts of the

45
00:03:05,223 --> 00:03:09,525
Kallang airport had all been
built on old graveyards.

46
00:03:09,525 --> 00:03:12,641
With the population
growing by 30,000 a year,

47
00:03:12,721 --> 00:03:17,684
the committee recommended that a
public crematorium be built as soon as

48
00:03:17,764 --> 00:03:22,025
possible and that space be saved
in the existing cemeteries.

49
00:03:22,025 --> 00:03:24,894
The committee was careful to say that it

50
00:03:24,974 --> 00:03:27,850
did not want cremation made compulsory.

51
00:03:27,850 --> 00:03:30,623
It noted that the
Chinese preferred burial,

52
00:03:30,703 --> 00:03:35,069
but believed they would come round
to cremation with encouragement,

53
00:03:35,149 --> 00:03:38,400
on the principle that the
living must come first.

54
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:42,830
Two days later, the Straits
Times reported that most sections

55
00:03:42,910 --> 00:03:47,050
of the Chinese community
supported a public crematorium.

56
00:03:47,050 --> 00:03:52,615
The government crematorium at
Mount Vernon was built in 1962,

57
00:03:52,695 --> 00:03:56,675
and a columbarium beside
it opened in 1976.

58
00:03:56,675 --> 00:04:00,539
Meanwhile a new municipal
cemetery had opened

59
00:04:00,619 --> 00:04:04,475
at Choa Chu Kang in
1947, far from the town.

60
00:04:04,475 --> 00:04:08,605
In the early 1970s the
city cemeteries took

61
00:04:08,685 --> 00:04:13,010
their last burials:
Bidadari in October 1972,

62
00:04:13,090 --> 00:04:17,300
and Bukit Brown and
Peck San Theng in 1973.

63
00:04:17,300 --> 00:04:20,378
Choa Chu Kang is now the only cemetery

64
00:04:20,458 --> 00:04:23,450
in Singapore still open for burials.

65
00:04:23,450 --> 00:04:28,475
Once a cemetery was closed, its
land could be taken for development.

66
00:04:28,475 --> 00:04:33,478
Peck San Theng was acquired
by the government in 1979,

67
00:04:33,558 --> 00:04:38,750
and in stages between 1982
and 1984 an estimated 100,000

68
00:04:38,830 --> 00:04:43,725
graves were exhumed to make
way for Bishan New Town.

69
00:04:43,725 --> 00:04:47,341
The clan association kept
a small part of the site,

70
00:04:47,421 --> 00:04:51,625
where it built a temple and a
columbarium for the remains.

71
00:04:51,625 --> 00:04:55,333
The Jewish cemetery on Thomson Road was

72
00:04:55,412 --> 00:04:59,200
cleared in 1985 for Novena MRT station.

73
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:03,432
The remains were moved to a
Jewish section at Choa Chu Kang,

74
00:05:03,512 --> 00:05:08,400
though some families had their
relatives reburied in Israel instead.

75
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:12,795
Between 2001 and 2006,
about 58,000 Christian

76
00:05:12,875 --> 00:05:17,766
and 68,000 Muslim graves
were exhumed at Bidadari,

77
00:05:17,846 --> 00:05:21,625
where a new housing estate now stands.

78
00:05:21,625 --> 00:05:25,252
The Muslim remains were
reburied at Choa Chu Kang,

79
00:05:25,332 --> 00:05:28,885
and the unclaimed Christian
remains were cremated

80
00:05:28,965 --> 00:05:32,450
and their ashes scattered
at sea in March 2008.

81
00:05:32,450 --> 00:05:35,989
Even Bukit Brown, which still stands,

82
00:05:36,069 --> 00:05:42,525
lost 3,746 graves to a new road
through the cemetery in the 2010s.

83
00:05:42,525 --> 00:05:46,700
As the cemeteries closed,
cremation became the norm.

84
00:05:46,700 --> 00:05:50,806
The government's crematorium
at Mandai opened in 1982,

85
00:05:50,886 --> 00:05:56,542
and when the old Mount Vernon crematorium
closed on the 30th of June 2004,

86
00:05:56,622 --> 00:06:00,575
a new crematorium at Mandai
took over the next day.

87
00:06:00,575 --> 00:06:04,766
According to the National
Environment Agency,

88
00:06:04,846 --> 00:06:08,088
cremations rose from 66 per cent of

89
00:06:08,168 --> 00:06:11,775
deaths in 1992 to 80 per cent in 2011.

90
00:06:11,775 --> 00:06:16,658
The ashes of most Singaporeans now
go to a niche in a columbarium,

91
00:06:16,738 --> 00:06:21,475
run by the government, a temple,
a church or a private company.

92
00:06:21,475 --> 00:06:24,925
A niche is not always forever either.

93
00:06:24,925 --> 00:06:28,596
The Mount Vernon columbarium
closed in September

94
00:06:28,676 --> 00:06:31,800
2018 to make way for the Bidadari estate,

95
00:06:31,880 --> 00:06:36,100
and its niches were moved
to Mandai and Choa Chu Kang.

96
00:06:36,100 --> 00:06:41,125
Many religious columbaria lease
their niches for a fixed term.

97
00:06:41,125 --> 00:06:46,037
The Franciscan columbarium, for
example, keeps urns for 30 years,

98
00:06:46,117 --> 00:06:50,646
which families can then extend
10 years at a time for a fee,

99
00:06:50,726 --> 00:06:56,025
and the Methodist Garden of Remembrance
leases its niches up to 2059.

100
00:06:56,025 --> 00:06:59,710
When my grandfather died,
and after him my grandmother,

101
00:06:59,790 --> 00:07:04,433
and later two of her sons, my family's
first thought each time was to

102
00:07:04,513 --> 00:07:08,825
place their ashes in the columbarium
at Saint Theresa's Church.

103
00:07:08,825 --> 00:07:13,350
Burial is still possible at
Choa Chu Kang, but not for good.

104
00:07:13,350 --> 00:07:18,074
Since the 1st of November 1998,
every grave there has been

105
00:07:18,154 --> 00:07:22,875
limited to 15 years, after
which the remains are exhumed.

106
00:07:22,875 --> 00:07:26,027
The National Environment Agency began

107
00:07:26,107 --> 00:07:29,950
exhuming the oldest
blocks in December 2004.

108
00:07:29,950 --> 00:07:35,452
When the expansion of Tengah
Air Base was announced in 2017,

109
00:07:35,532 --> 00:07:39,639
about 45,500 Chinese
graves and 35,000 Muslim

110
00:07:39,719 --> 00:07:44,650
graves at Choa Chu Kang were
to be exhumed in stages.

111
00:07:44,650 --> 00:07:48,258
In these exhumation
programmes, the agency exhumes

112
00:07:48,338 --> 00:07:51,871
each grave and cremates
the remains the same day,

113
00:07:51,951 --> 00:07:54,975
and it pays for a standard niche and urn.

114
00:07:54,975 --> 00:07:58,900
Families can also choose
to scatter the ashes.

115
00:07:58,900 --> 00:08:03,022
For faiths that require burial,
such as Islam and Judaism,

116
00:08:03,102 --> 00:08:06,500
the remains are reburied
in a much smaller plot,

117
00:08:06,580 --> 00:08:09,550
about an eighth the size of a full grave.

118
00:08:09,550 --> 00:08:14,243
Ashes can also be scattered at
sea, and the National Environment

119
00:08:14,323 --> 00:08:18,420
Agency plans a weekly ferry
service, as a two-year pilot

120
00:08:18,500 --> 00:08:22,975
from the second half of 2027,
to take families out to do it.

121
00:08:22,975 --> 00:08:27,950
My father's ashes were scattered
into the sea off Punggol End.

122
00:08:27,950 --> 00:08:34,064
In May 2021 the agency opened the Garden
of Peace at Choa Chu Kang Cemetery,

123
00:08:34,144 --> 00:08:37,975
Singapore's first inland
ash-scattering garden.

124
00:08:37,975 --> 00:08:41,725
It is a secular garden,
open to all faiths.

125
00:08:41,725 --> 00:08:46,032
Families scatter the finely
ground ashes along separate lanes,

126
00:08:46,112 --> 00:08:50,075
then water them so that they
sink into the soil beneath.

127
00:08:50,075 --> 00:08:54,793
Demand grew quickly, from
about 900 applications in 2021

128
00:08:54,873 --> 00:08:59,162
to about 2,000 in 2023,
and when the lanes fill up,

129
00:08:59,242 --> 00:09:04,125
the soil is moved to a designated
spot within the garden.

130
00:09:04,125 --> 00:09:07,567
A second garden, the Garden of Serenity,

131
00:09:07,647 --> 00:09:12,225
opened at the new Mandai
crematorium in August 2025.

132
00:09:12,225 --> 00:09:15,828
My mother's and my brother's
ashes were scattered at the

133
00:09:15,908 --> 00:09:19,525
Garden of Peace, and I have
been back there many times.

134
00:09:19,525 --> 00:09:24,150
It is out of the way if you do
not drive, but it is manageable.

135
00:09:24,150 --> 00:09:27,268
I like that the ashes are
washed into the gardens

136
00:09:27,348 --> 00:09:30,596
around the lanes, which
feels better to me than the

137
00:09:30,676 --> 00:09:34,200
uncertainty of a loved one
being somewhere in the sea.

138
00:09:34,200 --> 00:09:38,075
For Catholic families
the choice is not simple.

139
00:09:38,075 --> 00:09:42,633
The Church has allowed cremation
since 1963, but in 2016 the

140
00:09:42,713 --> 00:09:47,426
Vatican's instruction on burial
and cremation asked that ashes

141
00:09:47,506 --> 00:09:52,837
be kept in a sacred place, such as
a cemetery or a church columbarium,

142
00:09:52,917 --> 00:09:56,550
rather than scattered,
kept at home or divided.

143
00:09:56,550 --> 00:10:00,644
In 2023 it allowed a small
part of the ashes to be kept

144
00:10:00,724 --> 00:10:05,050
in a place of significance,
with the bishop's permission.

145
00:10:05,050 --> 00:10:08,877
In practice, many families
weigh this against cost,

146
00:10:08,957 --> 00:10:11,025
space and their own wishes.

147
00:10:11,025 --> 00:10:15,703
Among the Catholics I know, burial
versus cremation is still debated,

148
00:10:15,783 --> 00:10:20,391
but the characteristic Singaporean
pragmatism usually comes through,

149
00:10:20,471 --> 00:10:23,850
and more and more people
are choosing to scatter.

150
00:10:23,850 --> 00:10:26,025
Where it fits in the bigger story:

151
00:10:26,105 --> 00:10:30,535
Singapore's cemeteries show, more
plainly than almost anything else,

152
00:10:30,615 --> 00:10:33,600
what a small island
gives up to keep growing.

153
00:10:33,600 --> 00:10:37,713
In about a century the country
went from sprawling clan burial

154
00:10:37,793 --> 00:10:41,704
grounds to leased graves,
numbered niches and gardens where

155
00:10:41,784 --> 00:10:45,491
the ashes go back into the
soil, each step pushed by the

156
00:10:45,571 --> 00:10:49,900
shortage of land and made easier
by families who chose to adapt.

157
00:10:49,900 --> 00:10:54,000
Some of the old grounds survive
in fragments, at Bukit Brown,

158
00:10:54,080 --> 00:10:57,700
Fort Canning Green and the
Peck San Theng columbarium,

159
00:10:57,780 --> 00:11:00,852
but most of the dead of
old Singapore now lie,

160
00:11:00,932 --> 00:11:04,975
in one form or another, under
the city that grew over them.
